## THE FAIL — K2/Design-point-3's "Total" claim is false for realistic
## multi-month data (both living and healthcare)

K2's written acceptance text: living month rows "...sum to
`Metrics.LivingExpensesTotal`." Design point 3's written text: "Summary
tiles: Total (classified range total)..." — i.e., Total is defined as the
whole-RANGE classified total (Abs of ONE signed sum over the full range),
distinct from the per-month row figures (signed sum PER MONTH, then Abs,
per the same Design point 3 sentence).

The implementation instead sets `"Total": sum`, where `sum` is the
PRE-EXISTING scaffold loop's `sum += v` accumulator — i.e., SUM OF
PER-MONTH ABS VALUES, not Abs-of-whole-range-signed-sum. `cardMetrics
.LivingExpensesTotal` (== `math.Abs(livingOutflows.SumAmount())`,
metrics.go:433, a single Abs over the WHOLE classified range) is already
computed inside handleKPIDetail's own `cardMetrics := metrics.Calculate(...)`
call but is READ NOWHERE (grep "cardMetrics\." in handlers.go: only
`.ActualMonthly` and `.HealthcareActual` are ever read) — the range-total
figure Design point 3 asks for is computed and then thrown away.

Sum-of-per-month-Abs and Abs-of-whole-range-sum are mathematically
IDENTICAL only when every month in the range has the same net sign. They
DIVERGE whenever one month nets positive (refund-dominant) and another nets
negative (ordinary spend) — a realistic scenario this very codebase already
anticipates (the "Autopay Reversal"-style refund fixtures used throughout
this task's own test suite).

PROOF (rigorous, rule-6 style — computed via the actual code, not hand
arithmetic): built a 2-month living fixture (Jan: Rent -100 + refund +600 =
net +500; Feb: Rent -1000 = net -1000) and a probe that calls the SAME
internal pieces handleKPIDetail itself calls (`gatherDashboardCalcInputs` +
`metrics.Calculate`) on the identical data/range, reading
`cardMetrics.LivingExpensesTotal` directly:
  modal's rendered/JSON "Total" = 1500  (500 + 1000, sum of per-month Abs)
  cardMetrics.LivingExpensesTotal = 500  (Abs(500 - 1000) = Abs(-500))
These are NOT the same number for the IDENTICAL range and data. K2's own
text is violated.

Repeated for healthcare with an analogous fixture (Jan: -100 premium + 600
refund = net +500; Feb: -1000 premium = net -1000, coverage active
throughout):
  modal's Total = 1100  (500 + ... wait: per-month Abs sums to 500+... )
  actual observed modal Total = 1100 (500's Jan Abs=500 + Feb Abs=1000... )
  expected classified-range-total = 500 (Abs(500-1000))
Confirmed divergent for BOTH new kinds, not just living.

Why this survived attempt-1's checker-tests PASS and attempt-2's own K2
test: both use ONLY a SINGLE-MONTH fixture (Jan 2025, one month), where
sum-of-per-month-Abs and Abs-of-whole-range-sum are ALGEBRAICALLY IDENTICAL
by construction (only one term in the sum) — the fixture cannot distinguish
the two formulas no matter how carefully its output is checked.
checker-tests attempt 1 explicitly re-derived and confirmed "1300" against
metrics.Calculate on that SAME single-month fixture and called it settled;
it is settled only for single-month ranges.

## Scope
In scope: `handleKPIDetail`'s Total assembly for the two new kinds is code
this task added (the `"Total": sum` map entry, and the unused
`cardMetrics.LivingExpensesTotal`/implicit `.HealthcareTotal` this task's
own `cardMetrics := metrics.Calculate(...)` call already computes). Fixable
without exceeding KD1's file list (handlers.go, already touched). Governed
by K2's own written text and Design point 3's explicit "Total (classified
range total)" — not my own taste, not a beyond-scope pre-existing-code
finding.

## Recommended fix shape (not prescriptive)
Read the range total from `cardMetrics.LivingExpensesTotal` /
`math.Abs(healthcareOutflows.FilterByDateRange(...).SumAmount())` (or
equivalent already-computed `cardMetrics` healthcare total) for the "Total"
map key instead of the per-month-summed `sum` scaffold variable, for the
"living"/"healthcare" branches only — Low/High/MinMonth/MaxMonth may
legitimately keep using the per-month `values` array (Design point 3 does
not claim those equal any metrics.* figure).
